  "worked_solution": "To find $(\\frac{3}{2}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $3$ and position $2$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $(\\frac{3}{2})$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{1}{0}) &  & (\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{2}{0}) &  & (\\frac{2}{1}) &  & (\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  & (\\frac{3}{0}) &  & (\\frac{3}{1}) &  & (\\frac{3}{2}) &  & (\\frac{3}{3}) \\\\  &  &  & \u22ee &  & \u22ee &  & \u22ee &  & \u22ee &  & \u22ee\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(\\frac{3}{2})$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So,\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3}{2})=(\\frac{2}{1})+(\\frac{2}{2})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $(\\frac{2}{2}).$",
